WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.134
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:08.134 --> 00:00:09.050
SPEAKER: Good morning.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:13.300
Please welcome Jack Lo.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.901
JACK LO: Good morning,
everyone, and welcome to Next.

00:00:15.901 --> 00:00:17.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:19.490
Thank you.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:21.415
I hope you all
enjoyed the keynotes.

00:00:21.415 --> 00:00:23.540
As you heard, we've been
making a tremendous amount

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:25.850
of investment here
at Google to try

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:28.340
to help enterprises
come to the Cloud

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.740
and address your most critical,
most important business

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:33.130
challenges.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:34.910
The keynotes were
packed, as we all know.

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:36.830
There are a lot of people
still trickling in.

00:00:36.830 --> 00:00:39.620
Unfortunately, I'll have to
go through this a little bit

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.750
quicker so we'll get
you back on schedule

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.000
for all-important lunchtime.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.350
But again, my name is Jack.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.350
And I have the privilege and
the honor to speak about a topic

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:50.630
that I'm personally
very passionate about--

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:53.360
namely, how to help
enterprises bring together

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.360
your on-prem environments
with Google Cloud.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:04.190
So as you heard in
the keynotes, we

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:07.640
believe that there is this
nirvana ahead of building

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.350
cloud-native applications.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:12.500
And in fact, at Google,
over the past two decades

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.770
we've been building applications
that support billions of users.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.590
In fact, there are
eight applications

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:22.280
ranging from Search and
YouTube, Gmail, et cetera,

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.280
that actually have taught
us the value of building

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.400
cloud-native applications.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.280
Now, as we've moved
to public cloud,

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.650
we do believe that
this is ultimately

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:35.440
the end state that everyone
should be striving for.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.320
When you actually build
applications in this style,

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.650
not only do you have the
ability to have importable,

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:43.390
but as you containerize
applications,

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:46.900
you take the benefits of
operational efficiencies.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.360
You can actually
decouple applications

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.850
from operating system upgrades.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:54.810
You can help your developers
be more efficient.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.220
Often times, you're breaking
them into micro services

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:00.940
so individual teams can
work separately and upgrade

00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:02.950
on their own cadence
rather than having

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.734
to coordinate a large monolith.

00:02:05.734 --> 00:02:07.150
And in fact, these,
basically, are

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:10.720
some of the reasons that
this model, by increasing

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.110
your operational efficiency
and helping your agility,

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.870
this is really what drives
a lot of new directions

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:20.140
in Cloud and in
software development.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.390
But as much as we want to
go up to that direction,

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:25.630
the reality is that the
majority of applications

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:27.730
today are down in
what we would consider

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.560
this lower-left quadrant.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.160
They're sitting on-prem.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:35.320
They're classic applications,
large monoliths, not easy

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:39.670
to upgrade, not easy
to modify, sometimes

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:41.600
difficult to maintain.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.780
And so the question
is, what do we actually

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:46.490
do to address
these applications?

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:49.270
Ultimately, we want to
take these workloads

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:53.510
and try to find a way to
get to the upper right.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:57.000
Unfortunately, that's just
not so straightforward.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.670
You can have a whole
host of reasons

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.040
why your applications
might be difficult to move

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.000
to a new development model, or
even a new deployment model.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.840
A wholesale migration like this
may seem extremely daunting

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:10.160
for most enterprises.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:16.380
You know, you might say,
well, I'm a risk taker,

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:18.340
or I actually believe
in the future.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:20.460
And so I'm going to
try to rip and replace.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:23.790
This is one term that
oftentimes people use to say,

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.860
essentially I'm
going to start over.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.020
I actually have an
important application.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:29.490
I'm going to re-architect it.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.110
I'm going to
rewrite it so that I

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.690
can get these
opportunities that come

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.220
with the cloud-native
application

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.780
in the public cloud.

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:40.140
But given the practical
challenges and complexities

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.530
with such an approach,
this is not the path

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.240
that most of the
time you'll take.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.300
So let's look at a few more
incremental journeys to get

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:51.830
to this upper right nirvana.

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.320
So lift and shift,
you've probably all

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.660
heard about this
approach where we're

00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:59.570
taking existing applications
and largely moving

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:04.050
them as-is to the public cloud.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:07.470
Sometimes this ends up being
the most pragmatic approach.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.410
And the reasons might be
because the applications are

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:13.380
coming from a third party ISV.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:14.830
You may not have source code.

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:17.790
So it's not easy for you to
rewrite them or re-architect

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.820
them in a modern way.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:22.857
Or perhaps you actually
do have the source code,

00:04:22.857 --> 00:04:24.690
but these are applications
that were written

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.660
by developers many, many
years ago, most of whom

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.970
may not even be at
the company anymore.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:31.890
Or they're a little bit
brittle and fragile.

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:34.182
So you're worried about
actually can you actually make

00:04:34.182 --> 00:04:36.390
substantial changes because
the application's working

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:39.140
just fine today.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:42.580
So in these cases,
moving to a public cloud

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.260
can still bring you benefits.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.760
The question is,
how do you actually

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:48.160
make that migration
smooth and easy?

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:51.190
And later on in this talk,
I'll describe a few ways we've

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.710
been doing that here at Google.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.620
But even if you just move
the application as is,

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.110
the migration to
public cloud can still

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:01.120
bring you a lot of benefits.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:04.150
For example, you might be
running into a situation

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:06.910
where you have to upgrade
your physical infrastructure,

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:09.100
or turning down
your data centers,

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.360
and you have to make
a decision anyway

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:13.870
about where you're going
to run your workloads.

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:18.430
At this point, if you move
the applications to GCP,

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:22.377
you can still get the
benefits of efficiency

00:05:22.377 --> 00:05:24.460
because now you don't have
to worry about managing

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.080
the physical infrastructure.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:28.820
With capabilities like our live
migration and Google Compute

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:32.110
Engine, we handle the
upgrades of the servers

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:34.420
and the low-level
software on the machines

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:37.000
while the applications
continue to run.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:38.620
One great example
of this recently

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:42.070
was the processor
vulnerabilities, Spectre

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:43.360
and Meltdown.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:45.910
These were the largest
security vulnerabilities that

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:48.100
had been discovered in decades.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.757
We were able, on
Google Cloud, to be

00:05:49.757 --> 00:05:52.090
able to keep your applications
and workloads continually

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:55.600
running without actually having
to disrupt the applications

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:58.540
and make those patches to
the underlying software.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:02.400
That's something that
reduces an operational burden

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.120
from your staff.

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:10.401
Now, beyond lift
and shift, you might

00:06:10.401 --> 00:06:11.900
say, well, for some
of my workloads,

00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:14.420
I can take it one incremental
step further, what

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:16.430
you might term as
lift and modernize,

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.680
or lift and re-platform.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:21.800
And so what we mean by this
approach or this journey

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:24.710
is to take the step of,
say, a workload that's

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:28.950
running on-premises and then
moving it to a managed service.

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.730
So a great example of this might
be a database where you can now

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:35.000
move it from an on-premise
database to Cloud SQL,

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.920
or take your on-premise
Redis instance

00:06:36.920 --> 00:06:39.560
and move it to
Cloud Memory Store.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:42.350
With this path,
with this journey,

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:45.260
you no longer have to
manage the service itself.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:46.850
That's handled by Google.

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:49.160
And it allows you to just
move your application

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:51.080
without any code changes.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:54.860
Again, a slightly
lower-friction migration path

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:58.550
that's available to you to help
you on your journey to Cloud

00:06:58.550 --> 00:06:59.070
Native.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:05.040
Alternately, there
is another direction

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:07.672
which is to improve
first and then move.

00:07:07.672 --> 00:07:09.630
And by this what we mean
is thinking about some

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:11.960
of the applications
that you have today

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:15.660
and deciding to take that step
and start to containerize them.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:19.500
So separate the application
from the operating system,

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.140
as mentioned in the
keynotes, using Kubernetes

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:25.290
to start to orchestrate these
containers and the deployment

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:28.890
and management of
these applications.

00:07:28.890 --> 00:07:31.050
Most IT organizations
have already

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:33.360
been adopting Kubernetes
for at least some

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:35.760
of the applications
and services.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:39.240
And then once you've learned
the technology, learned

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:41.820
the deployment practices,
it becomes much easier

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:44.160
to take that next step
to move to the cloud.

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:52.640
Now, I'll mention that
yet another journey here,

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:56.510
in addition to lift and shift,
lift and improve, improve

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:58.860
and move, is build and connect.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:01.490
So this is a little bit more
about an architecture style

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:03.770
rather than a form of migration.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:05.570
And what I mean
by architecture is

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:07.730
some of your
complex applications

00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:10.650
and services may have
many, many components.

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:13.040
Some of that are going
to stay on premises.

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:15.290
Some of that will be
running on the cloud.

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:17.810
And in this kind of an
environment or architecture,

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:20.090
you'd be looking at how
to use things like Apigee

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:22.730
and developing API
gateways to connect

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:24.800
that various components
of your system

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:27.710
that span multiple locations.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:31.620
I'll talk briefly about
this later in the talk.

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:34.130
But at this point, the
thing that comes through

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:35.750
if you look at
all these journeys

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:40.679
is that it's a complex
path to get to the cloud.

00:08:40.679 --> 00:08:42.740
It can certainly
be very daunting.

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:46.430
Most of you live in a very
diverse, heterogeneous world,

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:50.960
where you have applications that
are these shrink-wrapped apps.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:53.060
Some of them you've
developed in-house,

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:54.806
others might be SaaS apps.

00:08:54.806 --> 00:08:56.180
And then on top
of that, you have

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:58.388
lots of different data
centers and environments, some

00:08:58.388 --> 00:09:01.430
that you own, some that may be
in a managed service provider,

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:02.900
some of that are in the cloud.

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:05.650
And you may be using
multiple clouds.

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:09.560
At Google, we recognize that
enterprises are complex.

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:12.800
And while we have a path that
we think is the right direction

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:17.060
to take, ultimately we want
to meet you where you are.

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:18.770
It's important to
let you run what

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:21.890
you want where you want
to satisfy your business

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:22.967
objectives.

00:09:27.740 --> 00:09:29.202
So how do you get started?

00:09:29.202 --> 00:09:30.910
I think an important
thing to think about

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:33.105
is, what are your goals?

00:09:33.105 --> 00:09:34.480
Are you trying to
move everything

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:36.670
as quickly as you
can to the cloud?

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:39.610
Or do you want to take a
more incremental approach?

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:42.250
As we meet with customers,
oftentimes what people look at

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:46.970
is, what are some of the easiest
applications to move first?

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:49.240
So pick some of your
test dev applications.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:51.220
But also start planning
an architecture that

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:54.940
allows you to move your more
complex workloads eventually,

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:58.150
so breaking it up
into waves and phases.

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:00.710
Part of the effort here is
also around understanding

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:03.610
first discovering what
applications you have.

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:07.090
How can you assess whether these
are good applications to move?

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:10.120
Do the planning and then
finally do the migration.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:12.550
And then once you've
migrated them, optimize them.

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:16.157
So there are multiple sessions
here at the conference

00:10:16.157 --> 00:10:18.490
where people go into more
detail about the programs that

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:21.460
are available through
partners, through PSO,

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:25.870
to help you with this
full migration process.

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:27.900
But now that we've talked
about some approaches

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:30.240
at a per-application
level, let's

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:33.690
take a step back and think
about the holistic picture.

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:38.040
In the keynote, [? Urs ?]
described the notion of hybrid.

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:41.220
And so ultimately, what you need
to have is the infrastructure

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:43.740
and framework to think
about how you manage

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:47.490
a diverse set of applications
across a diverse set

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:49.150
of locations.

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:54.120
And this is also a very critical
part of your cloud journey.

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:55.840
So what is hybrid?

00:10:55.840 --> 00:10:58.970
You know, I think if you ask all
the people, probably 500 people

00:10:58.970 --> 00:11:02.210
in the room, we might
get 400 different answers

00:11:02.210 --> 00:11:04.160
of exactly what hybrid means.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.810
It has a lot of different
interpretations.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:09.030
But for the purposes
of this talk,

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:12.230
I think where I want to focus
on is just the recognition

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:15.020
that you do have
multiple locations

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:18.350
and multiple applications
and services that need to run

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:20.720
in a location that suits you.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:24.320
And again, put simply, do
what you want where you want.

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:32.260
And what we found is that
this approach is something

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:35.360
that resonates with customers.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:38.500
This is a survey of cloud
users by IDC recently.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:44.390
And they found that 87%
of cloud users are hybrid.

00:11:44.390 --> 00:11:46.310
More than half of them
have multiple kinds

00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:49.550
of cloud deployments
in their organization.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:54.200
And a large fraction of
them, 40%, are cloud-first.

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:58.940
So a lot of these trends are
very likely to be accelerating.

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:02.460
Practically speaking,
hybrid is a reality.

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:04.790
It's a state that exists now.

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:06.652
It's going to
continue to increase.

00:12:06.652 --> 00:12:08.360
And it's going to be
a state that's going

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:09.720
to be here for years to come.

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:16.690
So how do you think about what
you want to do around hybrid?

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:18.730
One way is to break
it up into layers

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:22.150
in an overall architecture
starting from the network then

00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:25.870
looking at the application layer
and then the management layer.

00:12:25.870 --> 00:12:29.150
So lets look at each
of these in turn.

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:31.750
So networking obviously
is the underpinnings.

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:35.140
And in a sec, I will go into
some examples of how you might

00:12:35.140 --> 00:12:37.490
build your hybrid network.

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:39.020
At the application
layer, this is

00:12:39.020 --> 00:12:40.920
where there is a
lot of flexibility,

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:43.760
a lot of technology, a lot
of opportunity leveraging

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:47.300
the latest technologies like
containers and serverless.

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:49.010
As we heard in the
keynotes this morning,

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:51.860
the cloud services
platform and GKE on-prem

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:54.410
are examples of how you
actually build your applications

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:57.808
and services and spread across
your hybrid infrastructure.

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:04.600
And then on the highest layer,
thinking about management,

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:09.090
so lets go through a
couple of these starting

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:12.420
here, so networking.

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:18.510
So with networking Google
Cloud, one important step

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:20.700
in providing a
hybrid infrastructure

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:22.850
is figuring out how to
connect your on-premise data

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:25.750
center with Google Cloud.

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:28.480
So the Virtual
Private Cloud, or VPC,

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:32.500
is a way to actually create
an isolated network in GCP

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:35.740
for your own private access.

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:39.340
It allows you to make GCP
look like your own network.

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:41.470
But when you connect it
to your own data center,

00:13:41.470 --> 00:13:44.020
then it becomes
simply an extension

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:47.010
of your on-premises footprint.

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:49.410
But it's more than just
connecting a single data center

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:51.720
to one GCP region.

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:55.500
A single VPC can actually
span multiple regions

00:13:55.500 --> 00:13:58.560
across the globe
without having to go out

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:00.660
onto the public internet.

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:02.310
So why is that important?

00:14:02.310 --> 00:14:04.050
Well, for several reasons.

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:06.710
One, from that single
on-premise data center

00:14:06.710 --> 00:14:10.765
you've now been able to connect
to all of the Google regions.

00:14:10.765 --> 00:14:12.140
You don't actually
have to set up

00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:14.990
a separate different connection
from your data center

00:14:14.990 --> 00:14:18.080
to each of the regions that
you want to operate in.

00:14:18.080 --> 00:14:21.020
Secondly, it gives you much
lower and more predictable

00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:23.330
latencies when you
actually have to connect

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:26.060
across these regions,
especially for those services

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:29.090
that you have that are large
scale, global services,

00:14:29.090 --> 00:14:32.720
because you don't have to go
out into the public internet.

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:36.070
And then finally,
having a global VPC

00:14:36.070 --> 00:14:38.440
really helps you in
terms of manageability

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:40.990
because you can now
have a single VPC

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:44.410
policy that spans this network.

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:46.232
And from a management
standpoint,

00:14:46.232 --> 00:14:47.440
that simplifies your problem.

00:14:53.470 --> 00:14:55.650
So let's say you actually
wanted to take advantage

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:57.300
of these technologies.

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:01.370
What are some of the
steps that you would take?

00:15:01.370 --> 00:15:03.890
So there are three
different options

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:06.410
to connect your
data center to GCP.

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:09.980
The first is to use a more
traditional VPN-like approach.

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:17.240
So with Cloud VPN, you
connect your GCP project

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:19.970
to an on-premise VPN gateway.

00:15:19.970 --> 00:15:23.380
And then now you can
encrypt data in transit.

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:25.420
For some customers,
your requirements

00:15:25.420 --> 00:15:27.250
might be more substantial.

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:30.520
And so you could choose to go
down the dedicated interconnect

00:15:30.520 --> 00:15:32.240
route.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:35.810
With dedicated interconnect,
you bring your network

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:39.020
and you meet it at one of our
dedicated interconnect points

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:40.220
of presence.

00:15:40.220 --> 00:15:43.910
We have more than 70 dedicated
POPs across worldwide

00:15:43.910 --> 00:15:46.240
and are continuing
to add to them.

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:48.470
With each of these
interconnect points,

00:15:48.470 --> 00:15:54.130
you can get increments of 10
gigabits per second bandwidth.

00:15:54.130 --> 00:15:55.900
But for some
customers, perhaps you

00:15:55.900 --> 00:15:59.677
don't have such demanding
networking needs.

00:15:59.677 --> 00:16:01.510
And that's why we have
a third option, which

00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:03.910
is called partner interconnect.

00:16:03.910 --> 00:16:07.240
This might be a case where
you're not really close to,

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:09.490
or your data centers aren't,
sufficiently close to one

00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:11.050
of our Points of Presence.

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:13.710
And so what you do is you
actually work with a partner.

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:18.160
The partner has a peering
location with GCP.

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:23.370
And then you peer
with a partner.

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:26.980
This is a turnkey solution
integrated into the console.

00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:29.800
And it allows you to have a
more flexible set of networking

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:33.730
options, up to 10 gigabits per
second per VLAN all the way

00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:35.280
down to 50 megabits per second.

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:41.030
So that provides the
basic infrastructure

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:43.670
that you need for a
hybrid architecture.

00:16:43.670 --> 00:16:46.730
Moving up the stack to
the application layer,

00:16:46.730 --> 00:16:49.160
there are many other talks
at the conference where

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:50.840
people go into detail here.

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:52.610
We could spend hours
talking about what

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:56.660
you wouldn't want to build
for hybrid applications.

00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:58.220
I think here I just
want to highlight

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:03.200
a few of the basic principles,
namely around portability,

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:07.050
abstraction, and automation.

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:11.960
So the ideas here are you want
to have a set of designs that

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:15.079
allow services and
components to interoperate

00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:17.329
in a meaningful way,
and so standardizing

00:17:17.329 --> 00:17:21.182
on open source components,
open APIs are a key part of it.

00:17:21.182 --> 00:17:22.640
And then once you
do this, you want

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:25.260
to be able to automate and
be able to manage at scale.

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:32.620
And then finally moving up
to the management layer,

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:35.260
here you can choose to take
a variety of approaches

00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:37.630
depending on what you
may have standardized

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:40.090
in your environment and
what you may decide you

00:17:40.090 --> 00:17:42.550
want to actually change
in your environment.

00:17:42.550 --> 00:17:45.800
Basically, it's kind of
your appetite for change.

00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:49.000
So at the management layer,
one of the most critical pieces

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:53.140
of your stack is
around monitoring.

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:55.540
So you need to
have a way to have

00:17:55.540 --> 00:17:57.850
ongoing monitoring of the
health of your applications

00:17:57.850 --> 00:17:59.280
and services.

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:02.260
And Stackdriver provides
a suite of tools that

00:18:02.260 --> 00:18:04.569
give you operational awareness.

00:18:04.569 --> 00:18:06.610
There's a set of tools
for logging and monitoring

00:18:06.610 --> 00:18:09.220
the services that
are running inside

00:18:09.220 --> 00:18:11.140
of, say, virtual
machines or running

00:18:11.140 --> 00:18:13.177
in terms of other containers.

00:18:13.177 --> 00:18:15.010
And it doesn't matter
where they're running.

00:18:15.010 --> 00:18:16.030
They can be on GCP.

00:18:16.030 --> 00:18:17.370
They can be running on-premise.

00:18:17.370 --> 00:18:19.630
They can be running elsewhere.

00:18:19.630 --> 00:18:23.070
And this is one way to kind
of unify your management

00:18:23.070 --> 00:18:25.860
of your workloads.

00:18:25.860 --> 00:18:29.380
A second path is Kubernetes.

00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:32.530
As it was announced this
morning, with GKE on-prem,

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:36.160
we now have the ability to have
a single controlled experience

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:40.970
that manages across the
cloud and on premises.

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:43.660
So no matter where you run
them, on your data centers

00:18:43.660 --> 00:18:47.970
or in the cloud, you can
have a consistent experience.

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:51.360
And the third one is maybe
aimed at some of you who might

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:53.890
have a VMware environment.

00:18:53.890 --> 00:18:57.210
So you might have lots of
tools and practices built up

00:18:57.210 --> 00:18:59.710
around your VMware
infrastructure.

00:18:59.710 --> 00:19:01.170
We have been
partnering with VMware

00:19:01.170 --> 00:19:05.085
to build a plugin to fit
into that set of tools.

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:10.690
So we recently,
last week, announced

00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:14.410
that we've been working
together on a plugin

00:19:14.410 --> 00:19:18.280
for vRealize automation
and vRealize orchestrator.

00:19:18.280 --> 00:19:22.450
So for those of you who aren't
familiar with this environment,

00:19:22.450 --> 00:19:25.060
essentially what some
customers have done

00:19:25.060 --> 00:19:30.206
is created a portal or sort
of their own private cloud.

00:19:30.206 --> 00:19:31.330
And they create blueprints.

00:19:31.330 --> 00:19:34.210
This is one of the more common
use cases for these tools

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:36.610
where you've identified
if some end user wants

00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:38.230
to actually create
an application,

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:39.340
you click on a blueprint.

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:41.680
It automatically
deploys resources.

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:46.300
Now, for those people
who are using VRO or VRA,

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:50.080
the natural extension is to be
able to back those resources

00:19:50.080 --> 00:19:52.810
with resources in GCP.

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:55.540
And so this plugin
allows you to get

00:19:55.540 --> 00:19:58.300
some of the benefits of
cloud while continuing

00:19:58.300 --> 00:20:01.060
to use your existing
management infrastructure.

00:20:01.060 --> 00:20:02.470
So now you can use a blueprint.

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:05.060
The blueprint actually
creates virtual machines

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:06.820
in Google Compute Engine.

00:20:06.820 --> 00:20:08.740
And now those workloads
can take advantage

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:12.784
of things like basically
lower cost compute--

00:20:12.784 --> 00:20:14.200
because you have
the various forms

00:20:14.200 --> 00:20:18.290
of discounts like sustained use
or community use discounts--

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:20.320
the custom machine types.

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:22.540
And this environment is
now running and using

00:20:22.540 --> 00:20:24.460
resources in the cloud,
even though you're

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:27.854
using your existing on-premises
management infrastructure.

00:20:32.210 --> 00:20:35.120
So this plug-in is
in EAP right now.

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:37.040
If you're interested
in taking a look,

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:41.900
there's a sign up
for you to take part.

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:45.925
OK, folks.

00:20:45.925 --> 00:20:47.050
A second to take a picture.

00:20:52.130 --> 00:20:53.410
All right.

00:20:53.410 --> 00:20:56.590
So we've talked a little
bit about various journeys

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:58.090
that you would take
for applications

00:20:58.090 --> 00:20:59.512
to get to the cloud.

00:20:59.512 --> 00:21:01.720
We've talked a little bit
about a hybrid architecture

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:03.344
that you might want
to think about when

00:21:03.344 --> 00:21:05.000
you're coming in the cloud.

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:06.910
But you still have
to figure out how

00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:09.370
you can move those actual
workloads and that data

00:21:09.370 --> 00:21:11.360
as quickly as possible.

00:21:11.360 --> 00:21:13.085
So if you're going
down this path,

00:21:13.085 --> 00:21:14.710
what are some of the
biggest challenges

00:21:14.710 --> 00:21:15.751
you're going to run into?

00:21:18.200 --> 00:21:21.410
A recent survey showed that
most migration projects,

00:21:21.410 --> 00:21:24.420
unfortunately, aren't
super successful.

00:21:24.420 --> 00:21:27.490
About 94% of them
run into delays.

00:21:27.490 --> 00:21:29.070
They're over budget.

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:30.690
We've often heard
even of cases where

00:21:30.690 --> 00:21:32.852
people have moved
workloads to the cloud only

00:21:32.852 --> 00:21:35.310
to move them back because of
something they didn't realize,

00:21:35.310 --> 00:21:37.850
something they didn't
plan for properly.

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:39.590
Why is this hard?

00:21:39.590 --> 00:21:42.290
Well, a lot of organizations
have not just hundreds

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:45.770
but maybe thousands of
workloads and applications

00:21:45.770 --> 00:21:47.460
that they need to move.

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:49.399
So how do you handle the scale?

00:21:49.399 --> 00:21:51.440
How do you figure out
which applications to move,

00:21:51.440 --> 00:21:53.180
what the dependencies are?

00:21:53.180 --> 00:21:55.670
It's pretty daunting,
not to mention the fact

00:21:55.670 --> 00:21:57.260
that organizationally,
you will have

00:21:57.260 --> 00:21:59.199
different owners of
these applications

00:21:59.199 --> 00:22:01.490
and you may need to get their
approval in the migration

00:22:01.490 --> 00:22:03.670
process.

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:06.190
Another area of
complexity is they

00:22:06.190 --> 00:22:07.860
come from different sources.

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:10.830
Many of them could be running
in virtual machines on VMware.

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:12.637
They could be
running on physical.

00:22:12.637 --> 00:22:14.220
Some of them could
be in another cloud

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:17.630
and you want to
move them to GCP.

00:22:17.630 --> 00:22:20.164
Of course, we have
also complexity.

00:22:20.164 --> 00:22:21.705
Your most mission-critical
workload--

00:22:21.705 --> 00:22:24.870
say it's in SAP or
in Oracle database--

00:22:24.870 --> 00:22:27.590
these are applications
that are business critical.

00:22:27.590 --> 00:22:30.350
You do want to take advantage
of moving them to the cloud.

00:22:30.350 --> 00:22:34.040
But the problem is, you
can't take any business risk

00:22:34.040 --> 00:22:35.960
and you don't even want
to take any downtime,

00:22:35.960 --> 00:22:40.460
or at least keep that downtime
to an absolute minimum.

00:22:40.460 --> 00:22:44.420
So those concerns lead
into this question of risk.

00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:46.812
How can you actually
prepare yourself and test?

00:22:46.812 --> 00:22:48.520
And is there a way
that you can make sure

00:22:48.520 --> 00:22:51.040
that it's highly likely
that workloads succeed when

00:22:51.040 --> 00:22:54.550
you move them, but in the worst
case, if things don't go well,

00:22:54.550 --> 00:22:55.360
you can roll back?

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:02.000
Fortunately, we came
across a partner

00:23:02.000 --> 00:23:04.810
that we'd been working with
for a while called Velostrata.

00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:08.030
And we recently announced an
acquisition about a month ago

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:09.390
that closed.

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:12.140
They were already a partner
helping with migrations

00:23:12.140 --> 00:23:13.400
to Google Cloud.

00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:16.880
But we found that it's a very
complementary team, technology,

00:23:16.880 --> 00:23:18.050
and product.

00:23:18.050 --> 00:23:19.940
And really what they
are enabling us to do

00:23:19.940 --> 00:23:23.720
is provide an enterprise-grade
purpose-built solution

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:28.430
for helping accelerate the
migrations of workloads to GCP.

00:23:28.430 --> 00:23:31.520
Not only can they move from
on-premises to Google Cloud,

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:34.030
but you can also
move from AWS to GCP.

00:23:37.470 --> 00:23:39.620
So this technology
and this product

00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:43.070
is a real enabler to reducing
the friction involved

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:45.770
in these complex migrations.

00:23:45.770 --> 00:23:48.260
One of the key
capabilities is that it

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:51.340
allows you to move your
workloads very quickly,

00:23:51.340 --> 00:23:54.990
oftentimes with less than
10 minutes of downtime.

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:56.240
And that's pretty amazing.

00:23:56.240 --> 00:24:00.290
If you think about how you want
to actually reduce the impact

00:24:00.290 --> 00:24:02.060
and reduce the friction
of a migration,

00:24:02.060 --> 00:24:05.650
if you can reduce that downtime,
this is a critical advantage.

00:24:05.650 --> 00:24:07.400
So the typical migration,
what will happen

00:24:07.400 --> 00:24:10.010
is you'll have a bunch of
data, the disks associated

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:12.740
with your VMs, and you need
to copy that up to the cloud

00:24:12.740 --> 00:24:15.110
before you can actually migrate.

00:24:15.110 --> 00:24:18.379
And if it's a large VM with,
say, a terabyte virtual disk,

00:24:18.379 --> 00:24:19.670
that's going to take some time.

00:24:19.670 --> 00:24:21.230
That could take hours.

00:24:21.230 --> 00:24:22.670
Now you multiply
that by hundreds

00:24:22.670 --> 00:24:24.710
of workloads or
thousands, and you can see

00:24:24.710 --> 00:24:28.070
how long this process can take.

00:24:28.070 --> 00:24:29.960
The key insight from
the Velostrata team

00:24:29.960 --> 00:24:35.920
was to look at how you
decouple compute from storage.

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:43.630
And so the idea is that actually
you can stream the storage.

00:24:43.630 --> 00:24:46.000
So essentially what happens
is you take a short downtime

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:47.560
period at the beginning.

00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:50.110
And you start bringing over
the most critical pieces

00:24:50.110 --> 00:24:51.042
of the storage.

00:24:51.042 --> 00:24:52.750
And there's a lot of
intelligence and WAN

00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:55.970
optimization in this
technology that allows

00:24:55.970 --> 00:24:58.040
you to start that process.

00:24:58.040 --> 00:25:00.250
And then once you've moved
those critical pieces,

00:25:00.250 --> 00:25:03.340
you can start running
your workload in the cloud

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:07.005
while the rest of the data gets
migrated in the background.

00:25:10.379 --> 00:25:12.670
You know, they say a picture
is worth a thousand words.

00:25:12.670 --> 00:25:14.860
So let's take a
look at this graph.

00:25:14.860 --> 00:25:16.480
What it shows here is--

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:19.270
this is from a customer example.

00:25:19.270 --> 00:25:21.460
This is showing
time on the x-axis.

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:24.730
And y is both the number
of VMs that are running--

00:25:24.730 --> 00:25:26.480
sorry, that have been
moved to the cloud--

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:30.460
as well as in green the amount
of data that's been migrating.

00:25:30.460 --> 00:25:33.340
So this customer chose
to make two-week sprints

00:25:33.340 --> 00:25:35.830
to actually do the migration.

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:38.080
And you can see that
there's a step function.

00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:39.870
In the first week
what they would do--

00:25:39.870 --> 00:25:42.670
just to kind of blow into
one of these sprints--

00:25:42.670 --> 00:25:45.220
they did some discovery
planning, so picking up,

00:25:45.220 --> 00:25:48.340
say, 75 VMs that they
actually want to migrate.

00:25:48.340 --> 00:25:51.160
So figure out which ones they
are, kind of plan internally

00:25:51.160 --> 00:25:55.180
how they would move them,
actual scheduling the migration.

00:25:55.180 --> 00:25:58.780
And then in the second week,
do the actual migration.

00:25:58.780 --> 00:26:00.700
Now, what you can see
in this blown-up zoom

00:26:00.700 --> 00:26:04.930
is that most of the VMs
were moved and running

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:07.685
in the cloud in hours.

00:26:07.685 --> 00:26:09.810
Meanwhile, the data is
streaming in the background.

00:26:09.810 --> 00:26:12.059
And in fact, it took about
three days for all the data

00:26:12.059 --> 00:26:13.310
to get there.

00:26:13.310 --> 00:26:15.490
But in this time,
the customer was

00:26:15.490 --> 00:26:18.130
able to actually start running
these applications again

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:20.390
and not impact their business.

00:26:20.390 --> 00:26:22.349
And when you think
about the amount of time

00:26:22.349 --> 00:26:23.890
you have to do
migrations, this means

00:26:23.890 --> 00:26:26.800
you can easily do a migration
of a large number of workloads

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:31.660
over a weekend
rather than trying

00:26:31.660 --> 00:26:33.970
to look at many, many days
of moving a lot of data.

00:26:38.470 --> 00:26:40.840
So why is this important?

00:26:40.840 --> 00:26:45.090
Well, one, minimizing
downtime and then taking

00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:46.930
advantage of other
capabilities in the tool

00:26:46.930 --> 00:26:50.310
set to be able to validate
and increase the confidence

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:53.360
that your workloads
are successful.

00:26:53.360 --> 00:26:56.700
As I mentioned earlier,
oftentimes less than 10 minutes

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:58.610
and you're up and running.

00:26:58.610 --> 00:27:00.380
You save a lot of manpower.

00:27:00.380 --> 00:27:04.160
You accelerate the
process of migration.

00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:06.710
And this actually greatly
increases the likelihood

00:27:06.710 --> 00:27:09.110
that a migration project will
be successful because you

00:27:09.110 --> 00:27:11.650
can stay under budget.

00:27:11.650 --> 00:27:14.290
But earlier I mentioned
one of the challenges that

00:27:14.290 --> 00:27:17.680
was around risk and complexity.

00:27:17.680 --> 00:27:21.701
What this technology also
allows you to do is roll back.

00:27:21.701 --> 00:27:23.950
Unfortunately, there's a
conflicting deep dive session

00:27:23.950 --> 00:27:27.700
right now going on about
the Velostrata technology.

00:27:27.700 --> 00:27:30.250
So hopefully you can see
a video or look at some

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:31.630
of the collateral online.

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:33.880
But there's also the
ability to roll back

00:27:33.880 --> 00:27:36.560
because while we're streaming
the data to the cloud,

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:38.800
there's still data for that
virtual machine running

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:39.796
on premises.

00:27:39.796 --> 00:27:41.170
And if you find,
for some reason,

00:27:41.170 --> 00:27:45.580
that maybe you forgot to move
a workload that it depends on

00:27:45.580 --> 00:27:47.440
or you have some
other challenge,

00:27:47.440 --> 00:27:50.080
you always have the
ability to move back.

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:53.170
And that safety net,
that insurance policy,

00:27:53.170 --> 00:27:56.140
is very valuable when it
comes to actually doing

00:27:56.140 --> 00:27:58.510
these large-scale migrations.

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:01.750
And ultimately, now by getting
these migrations to happen,

00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:06.020
you can get to ramp up your
cloud usage much more quickly.

00:28:09.790 --> 00:28:12.550
Now, in addition to migrating
your virtual machines,

00:28:12.550 --> 00:28:15.540
you may actually also
have a lot of data

00:28:15.540 --> 00:28:17.850
that needs to be migrated.

00:28:17.850 --> 00:28:21.030
The data transfer
appliance is one solution

00:28:21.030 --> 00:28:24.360
that's available to
help take, if you have,

00:28:24.360 --> 00:28:26.794
say, hundreds of
terabytes of data

00:28:26.794 --> 00:28:28.710
that you need to move
to the cloud in addition

00:28:28.710 --> 00:28:31.830
to your virtual
machines, you just

00:28:31.830 --> 00:28:34.417
can't afford to actually
move all that data.

00:28:34.417 --> 00:28:36.750
This is not necessarily so
much for the virtual machines

00:28:36.750 --> 00:28:38.400
in the Velostrata
case, but it could

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:40.680
be because you have
some data warehouse

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:42.900
or other large amounts
of data that you

00:28:42.900 --> 00:28:45.930
need to be able to use
while you're in the cloud.

00:28:45.930 --> 00:28:50.370
So the transfer appliance
comes in 2U and 4U form

00:28:50.370 --> 00:28:55.320
factors with 100 terabyte
and 480 terabyte versions.

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:57.720
The larger one can handle
up to roughly about

00:28:57.720 --> 00:29:00.060
a petabyte of data compressed.

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:03.210
Basically, it's as simple
as going to the console,

00:29:03.210 --> 00:29:05.220
requesting a transfer appliance.

00:29:05.220 --> 00:29:05.760
You get it.

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:07.440
You rack it.

00:29:07.440 --> 00:29:10.980
You fill it up with data
and then ship it back to us.

00:29:10.980 --> 00:29:14.720
Basically, this is a solution
where you can actually

00:29:14.720 --> 00:29:16.470
have it sit in the
data center for a while

00:29:16.470 --> 00:29:18.271
and then bring it
back to Google Cloud.

00:29:21.220 --> 00:29:23.290
So now that we've
talked a little bit

00:29:23.290 --> 00:29:25.540
about some of the
migration challenges

00:29:25.540 --> 00:29:28.450
and some of the solutions
that are available,

00:29:28.450 --> 00:29:30.400
I wanted to bring
one of our customers

00:29:30.400 --> 00:29:32.600
from Cardinal Health,
Jon Latshaw, who

00:29:32.600 --> 00:29:35.140
is the Vice President of
Cloud Services at Cardinal,

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:37.870
and have him talk a little
bit about the company and some

00:29:37.870 --> 00:29:40.060
of the work they've
been doing with Google.

00:29:40.060 --> 00:29:40.870
Jon?

00:29:40.870 --> 00:29:42.286
JON LATSHAW: Thanks
for having me.

00:29:45.770 --> 00:29:47.770
JACK LO: So Jon, can you
first tell the audience

00:29:47.770 --> 00:29:49.936
and tell us a little bit
about Cardinal Health, what

00:29:49.936 --> 00:29:51.622
you guys do?

00:29:51.622 --> 00:29:53.080
JON LATSHAW: We do
a lot of things.

00:29:53.080 --> 00:29:56.415
But in summary, we try to make
health care more efficient

00:29:56.415 --> 00:29:58.300
and cost effective.

00:29:58.300 --> 00:30:01.770
We're a Fortune 15 company
with about $130 billion

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:08.800
in revenue, about 700 sites,
and a lot of applications,

00:30:08.800 --> 00:30:10.690
not to be too specific.

00:30:10.690 --> 00:30:12.610
JACK LO: Yeah.

00:30:12.610 --> 00:30:13.870
So--

00:30:13.870 --> 00:30:15.769
JON LATSHAW: One other
point, if any of you

00:30:15.769 --> 00:30:17.560
have had the misfortune
to be in a hospital

00:30:17.560 --> 00:30:19.990
or need to get a prescription
for some sort of illness,

00:30:19.990 --> 00:30:21.940
you've probably
used our services

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:25.635
just going to a CVS or
some sort of hospital.

00:30:25.635 --> 00:30:27.010
JACK LO: One of
the things when I

00:30:27.010 --> 00:30:29.290
was visiting you guys
recently, hearing the stories

00:30:29.290 --> 00:30:31.810
about the kind of on-demand
nature of the business

00:30:31.810 --> 00:30:34.834
you're in, like someone
ordering something today

00:30:34.834 --> 00:30:36.250
for a patient that
has a procedure

00:30:36.250 --> 00:30:39.097
tomorrow, and critical services
that you have to deliver

00:30:39.097 --> 00:30:40.180
to make sure that happens.

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:42.629
JON LATSHAW: It's hard to
plan emergencies, yeah.

00:30:42.629 --> 00:30:44.170
JACK LO: So can you
talk a little bit

00:30:44.170 --> 00:30:48.430
about what prompted Cardinal's
migration to the cloud?

00:30:48.430 --> 00:30:50.110
JON LATSHAW: Yeah.

00:30:50.110 --> 00:30:52.880
In a nutshell, it's
speed and efficiency.

00:30:52.880 --> 00:30:55.750
So we-- our business
is hard to predict.

00:30:55.750 --> 00:30:58.660
Wouldn't think that from
if you look at health care.

00:30:58.660 --> 00:31:02.185
But it's actually a large
component of our business

00:31:02.185 --> 00:31:06.040
is pharmaceutical distribution,
so where drugs need to be,

00:31:06.040 --> 00:31:08.310
where generics might launch.

00:31:08.310 --> 00:31:11.320
And so a lot of that requires
us to turn on a dime.

00:31:11.320 --> 00:31:12.860
And with traditional
infrastructure,

00:31:12.860 --> 00:31:16.800
it's just hard to do,
whereas, conversely, with GCP,

00:31:16.800 --> 00:31:20.830
I can spin up, spin down,
all at a moment's notice.

00:31:20.830 --> 00:31:26.240
And then although we have
a $130 billion in revenue,

00:31:26.240 --> 00:31:28.420
we actually run on
grocery store margins.

00:31:28.420 --> 00:31:30.380
So every penny counts.

00:31:30.380 --> 00:31:33.125
So we've found that running
in the cloud, if you do it

00:31:33.125 --> 00:31:34.750
the right way, can
be considerably more

00:31:34.750 --> 00:31:37.717
cost-effective than
doing it ourselves.

00:31:37.717 --> 00:31:40.300
JACK LO: So with that in mind,
I mean, can you talk about some

00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:43.270
of the best practices, or
perhaps lessons learned

00:31:43.270 --> 00:31:44.840
that you guys have experienced?

00:31:44.840 --> 00:31:46.060
JON LATSHAW: Yeah.

00:31:46.060 --> 00:31:49.160
To kind of go back a little
bit in your presentation,

00:31:49.160 --> 00:31:52.460
if you haven't heard of
Velostrata, go check it out.

00:31:52.460 --> 00:31:53.420
It's the real deal.

00:31:53.420 --> 00:31:55.810
We were one of their
first customers.

00:31:55.810 --> 00:31:59.410
Makes a tremendous difference
if you're doing any kind of lift

00:31:59.410 --> 00:32:01.780
and shift or lift and
lately optimize migrations,

00:32:01.780 --> 00:32:03.710
there's a lot of
that we're doing.

00:32:03.710 --> 00:32:05.800
And it really works
as advertised.

00:32:05.800 --> 00:32:08.590
It sounded a lot like magic to
me when they first pitched it.

00:32:08.590 --> 00:32:13.010
And they've proven to be a
great partner and it works.

00:32:13.010 --> 00:32:20.440
So on that, we're about a
third of the way through,

00:32:20.440 --> 00:32:23.450
and we've learned
a lot of things.

00:32:23.450 --> 00:32:25.680
There's one thing
that I would say--

00:32:25.680 --> 00:32:28.460
the technology is not
the hardest part of this.

00:32:28.460 --> 00:32:31.530
So while it is challenging
in some aspects,

00:32:31.530 --> 00:32:33.360
don't worry about that so much.

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:36.895
What I would say is, get your
house in order while you're

00:32:36.895 --> 00:32:37.770
contemplating moving.

00:32:37.770 --> 00:32:39.660
Move some non-prod workloads.

00:32:39.660 --> 00:32:41.940
But before you move
production, make

00:32:41.940 --> 00:32:45.420
sure that your CMDB is
accurate and up to date.

00:32:45.420 --> 00:32:47.280
We had a very complete CMDB.

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:50.200
We could go through and say
all the fields were filled out.

00:32:50.200 --> 00:32:52.830
But when we wanted to gain
intelligence about our systems,

00:32:52.830 --> 00:32:55.440
like what systems were part
of a business process, what

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:57.930
applications needed to talk
to what applications on what

00:32:57.930 --> 00:33:00.981
ports, it wasn't
reliable because we never

00:33:00.981 --> 00:33:01.980
had to use it like that.

00:33:01.980 --> 00:33:06.810
So spend some time getting
that stuff in order.

00:33:06.810 --> 00:33:09.450
And be sure you can answer
the right questions about what

00:33:09.450 --> 00:33:12.010
needs to talk to what.

00:33:12.010 --> 00:33:14.630
I would say be friends
with your finance people.

00:33:14.630 --> 00:33:17.930
This change is going
to have a ripple effect

00:33:17.930 --> 00:33:21.510
of substantial changes
through the finance team.

00:33:21.510 --> 00:33:24.514
They need to be prepared
for direct chargebacks.

00:33:24.514 --> 00:33:26.180
You don't have to do
direct chargebacks.

00:33:26.180 --> 00:33:26.950
We chose to.

00:33:26.950 --> 00:33:28.770
It drives the right behavior.

00:33:28.770 --> 00:33:32.510
But it is a massive
adjustment for them.

00:33:32.510 --> 00:33:34.970
Lastly, partner up.

00:33:34.970 --> 00:33:39.380
Maybe buy your CSO bottle
of their favorite beverage

00:33:39.380 --> 00:33:42.470
repeatedly.

00:33:42.470 --> 00:33:46.640
Security folks by their
nature are extremely cautious.

00:33:46.640 --> 00:33:49.040
Google has an enormous
emphasis on security.

00:33:49.040 --> 00:33:50.790
But it's not only
their responsibility.

00:33:50.790 --> 00:33:52.790
It's a joint responsibility.

00:33:52.790 --> 00:33:58.220
And we've spent a lot of
time on bringing the security

00:33:58.220 --> 00:34:02.410
folks along, getting our CSO on
board, getting an endorsement.

00:34:02.410 --> 00:34:03.890
So that's kind of in a nutshell.

00:34:03.890 --> 00:34:07.760
If I had it to do over again,
I would spend more time upfront

00:34:07.760 --> 00:34:11.750
on those things and less time
worrying about the technology.

00:34:11.750 --> 00:34:12.590
JACK LO: Great.

00:34:12.590 --> 00:34:14.480
So can you also
talk a little bit

00:34:14.480 --> 00:34:17.489
about your experience with
Google Cloud, specifically?

00:34:17.489 --> 00:34:22.020
What has kind of stood
out with that partnership?

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:24.230
JON LATSHAW: A couple
of things there.

00:34:24.230 --> 00:34:25.909
One, they've been
a great partner.

00:34:25.909 --> 00:34:30.230
So their product teams
have been very curious.

00:34:30.230 --> 00:34:31.949
We've gotten access to that.

00:34:31.949 --> 00:34:33.860
So for those that are
kind of just dipping

00:34:33.860 --> 00:34:36.469
the toe in the water,
if you've had experience

00:34:36.469 --> 00:34:39.710
with on-premise companies
like, I don't know,

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:42.170
a database company
that begins with an O

00:34:42.170 --> 00:34:44.880
or something like
that, and you say,

00:34:44.880 --> 00:34:48.320
hey, it'd be great to have this
feature in your next release,

00:34:48.320 --> 00:34:51.384
maybe in a year, two
years it might come out.

00:34:51.384 --> 00:34:53.800
We've had the experience of
working with our product teams

00:34:53.800 --> 00:34:57.726
to say, hey, on your API
gateway, we would like this.

00:34:57.726 --> 00:34:58.850
And they're like, OK, cool.

00:34:58.850 --> 00:35:02.610
And literally six weeks
later, we have that feature.

00:35:02.610 --> 00:35:05.210
So they've been extremely
responsive, extremely

00:35:05.210 --> 00:35:08.790
curious on what it takes
for us to move to the Cloud.

00:35:08.790 --> 00:35:10.620
And it's not an
unselfish investment.

00:35:10.620 --> 00:35:14.450
I mean, we're a lot like
every other Fortune 500.

00:35:14.450 --> 00:35:17.910
We have the widest variety
of systems you can imagine.

00:35:17.910 --> 00:35:21.200
And if Google invests
in making us successful,

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:25.580
then that just paves the
way for everyone else.

00:35:25.580 --> 00:35:28.700
I've found their
infrastructure, their network

00:35:28.700 --> 00:35:32.690
to be fast and reliable, more
so than other cloud providers

00:35:32.690 --> 00:35:33.690
we've looked at.

00:35:33.690 --> 00:35:37.010
Live migration's a
big feature for us.

00:35:37.010 --> 00:35:39.140
We did some things
internally that are

00:35:39.140 --> 00:35:41.390
kind of unnatural
acts, but it was

00:35:41.390 --> 00:35:46.040
to save on licensing fees
for some WebSphere products

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:51.980
where we would create a VM that
had two CPUs but 48 gig of RAM.

00:35:51.980 --> 00:35:54.770
And we did that because we
wanted to save on licensing.

00:35:54.770 --> 00:35:56.690
So in some cases, we're
going to unwind that

00:35:56.690 --> 00:35:58.856
and we're going to do it
the right way in the cloud.

00:35:58.856 --> 00:35:59.940
Some cases, we can't.

00:35:59.940 --> 00:36:03.800
And so their custom machine
size gives us that flexibility

00:36:03.800 --> 00:36:06.320
to do that instead of
kind of the t-shirt sizing

00:36:06.320 --> 00:36:09.200
that other cloud providers have.

00:36:09.200 --> 00:36:10.010
JACK LO: Great.

00:36:10.010 --> 00:36:11.000
Well, thank you so much, Jon.

00:36:11.000 --> 00:36:11.958
JON LATSHAW: Thank you.

00:36:11.958 --> 00:36:13.324
JACK LO: Appreciate it.

00:36:13.324 --> 00:36:18.534
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:18.534 --> 00:36:19.950
It's great to have
the opportunity

00:36:19.950 --> 00:36:22.410
of fantastic customers
like Jon and his team,

00:36:22.410 --> 00:36:24.240
and look forward
to collaborating

00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:28.310
further on their continued
journey into the cloud.

00:36:28.310 --> 00:36:32.545
So before I wrap up here,
I want to just highlight

00:36:32.545 --> 00:36:33.420
a number of sessions.

00:36:33.420 --> 00:36:35.169
There is actually
probably many other ones

00:36:35.169 --> 00:36:37.370
that are related to
the topics that we've

00:36:37.370 --> 00:36:39.659
talked about where you can
get a little bit deeper.

00:36:39.659 --> 00:36:41.450
There are some customers
who have also-- we

00:36:41.450 --> 00:36:45.350
can talk about their
experiences moving to GCP.

00:36:45.350 --> 00:36:47.960
Or if you want to look at
other different technologies

00:36:47.960 --> 00:36:50.560
and products that have
done migrations to Cloud,

00:36:50.560 --> 00:36:51.980
here's a few sessions.

00:36:51.980 --> 00:36:54.490
And as I mentioned earlier,
unfortunately that 112 session

00:36:54.490 --> 00:36:55.610
is going on right now.

00:37:00.420 --> 00:37:05.570
So I just want to leave you all
with a few important takeaways.

00:37:05.570 --> 00:37:09.410
I think what I would try to do
is paint the journeys that you

00:37:09.410 --> 00:37:10.700
might want to take.

00:37:10.700 --> 00:37:13.010
And it's really
important to recognize

00:37:13.010 --> 00:37:16.140
that this modernization
of IT is important.

00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:18.800
There's a great opportunity
here to improve efficiencies

00:37:18.800 --> 00:37:22.490
and help you accelerate
your business,

00:37:22.490 --> 00:37:24.380
rather than having IT
infrastructure just

00:37:24.380 --> 00:37:26.420
be a lot about cost.

00:37:26.420 --> 00:37:30.050
But ultimately, Google is
here to meet you where you we.

00:37:30.050 --> 00:37:32.870
Have a vision for where we
want it to take everyone.

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:35.990
But along the way, everyone
might have a slightly different

00:37:35.990 --> 00:37:37.610
journey.

00:37:37.610 --> 00:37:41.240
Second is hybrid is
just the reality.

00:37:41.240 --> 00:37:42.740
It is going to
require rethinking

00:37:42.740 --> 00:37:44.240
a little bit about
your architecture

00:37:44.240 --> 00:37:47.015
from a network to a management
to an application standpoint,

00:37:47.015 --> 00:37:48.890
but it's important to
have a vision for where

00:37:48.890 --> 00:37:52.580
you want to go because this
is a reality that's here

00:37:52.580 --> 00:37:55.520
for many, many years ahead.

00:37:55.520 --> 00:37:58.710
And then finally,
the journey is long.

00:37:58.710 --> 00:37:59.930
It's complex.

00:37:59.930 --> 00:38:02.160
But we're here to
support you, whether it's

00:38:02.160 --> 00:38:05.060
through PSO, through the
many partners we have here

00:38:05.060 --> 00:38:07.040
at the conference, from
the engineering teams,

00:38:07.040 --> 00:38:08.810
from the customer teams.

00:38:08.810 --> 00:38:11.490
We're here to help
you be successful.

00:38:11.490 --> 00:38:15.110
There's a lot of resources
and tools and guides.

00:38:15.110 --> 00:38:19.930
And so please reach out if you
need help with your migration.

00:38:19.930 --> 00:38:20.850
And thank you.

